Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?feat:=20super=20admin=20moderation=20=E2=80=94?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20cascade=20user=20delete=20+=20fix=20broken=20comment/car=20m?=
 =?UTF-8?q?oderation?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Cascade user delete: DELETE /api/v1/admin/users/{id}?cascade=true now
optionally removes everything tied to an account, scoped carefully per
role so it never touches anyone else's data — favorites, likes,
follows, comments, car requests, and notifications for any role; for
DEALER_ADMIN also their dealer organization, every car they listed,
expense records, sale transactions, and staff accounts (staff users
soft-deleted, not hard-deleted, so nothing else breaks). Message/
conversation history is deliberately left alone even with cascade, so
deleting one person doesn't erase the other party's side of a chat.
Logs every cascade deletion with counts to admin_deletion_logs.
cascade=False (default) keeps the existing simple soft-delete behavior
unchanged.

Found and fixed two real, pre-existing bugs while building this:
1. admin_delete_car's comment cleanup, and the standalone
   admin_delete_comment endpoint, both queried db['comments'] — a
   collection that doesn't exist. Real comments live in
   db['car_comments'] (per app/utils/comments_service.py). This meant
   admin comment deletion has always silently 404'd on every real
   comment, and deleting a car never actually cleaned up its comments.
2. The public comment-delete endpoint always rejected deleting anyone
   else's comment, even for admins — but the car detail page already
   shows a delete button to admins on every comment. Added an is_admin
   bypass so that button actually works instead of silently 403'ing.
